\subsection{Testing the repeated fragment's probability implication}
This record repeats the preceding bank question and is flagged as unrecoverable. The primary report omits its model. We do not reconstruct a new bank problem; instead we test the logical inference that absence of guaranteed avoidance necessarily means almost-sure eventual crossing. Let $X_t=x+\mu t+\sigma W_t$ with $x,\mu,\sigma>0$, and give this illustrative model only the one available policy.
\begin{proposition}
The probability that $X_t$ ever becomes strictly negative is $\exp(-2\mu x/\sigma^2)$, strictly between zero and one. Thus this one-policy model has no policy guaranteeing avoidance almost surely, but failure is not almost sure either.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Set $\lambda=2\mu/\sigma^2$. The process $Z_t=e^{-\lambda X_t}$ is a martingale: by independent Gaussian increments its conditional multiplier over duration $h$ has expectation $\exp(-\lambda\mu h+\lambda^2\sigma^2h/2)=1$.
Fix $L>x$ and let $\tau$ be the first exit from $(0,L)$. This time is finite almost surely. Indeed, conditional on not having exited by an integer time, the next unit increment $\mu+\sigma(W_{j+1}-W_j)$ has a fixed positive probability of exceeding $L$, which forces an exit by that next integer; independence bounds the probability of no exit for $m$ such intervals by $(1-p)^m$ for some $p>0$.
By the bounded-stopping martingale theorem, first at $t\wedge\tau$ and then by bounded convergence, $\mathbb E Z_\tau=e^{-\lambda x}$. Bounded convergence applies because the stopped continuous path stays in $[0,L]$, so $e^{-\lambda L}\le Z_{t\wedge\tau}\le1$. Continuity makes the exit location either 0 or $L$. If $p_L$ is the probability of exiting at 0, then
\[
e^{-\lambda x}=p_L+(1-p_L)e^{-\lambda L},
\qquad p_L=\frac{e^{-\lambda x}-e^{-\lambda L}}{1-e^{-\lambda L}}.
\]
As $L\to\infty$, the events of hitting 0 before $L$ increase to the event of ever hitting 0: a continuous path that hits 0 at finite time has a finite maximum before that hit. Hence this event has probability $e^{-\lambda x}$. Applying the same argument after translating the threshold to $-\delta$, $\delta>0$, gives probability $e^{-\lambda(x+\delta)}$ of ever reaching $-\delta$. The event of ever being negative is the increasing union of these events as $\delta\downarrow0$ along $1,1/2,1/3,\ldots$, proving the claimed strict-crossing probability.
\end{proof}
The failed inference confuses ``no policy has crossing probability zero'' with ``every policy has crossing probability one.'' The example separates these assertions without deciding either for the omitted bank model. In this example the crossing time also has infinite unconditional expectation because it is infinite with positive probability, so maximizing an unqualified expected delay would not necessarily be a useful objective.

